---
title: 'Key Features'
description: 'Overview of popular AG Charts features with code snippets. Learn how to use key Community features, customise themes and explore advanced Enterprise features.'
---

This page provides an overview of and introduction to popular features available in AG Charts. Learn how to use Community features, configure and customise themes, and get started with Enterprise features.

{% note %}
The following sections assume a level of familiarity with common Charts concepts. If you're new to Charts in general, we recommend starting with our [Introductory Tutorial](./create-a-basic-chart/) instead.
{% /note %}

## Displaying Data

{% chartExampleRunner title="Configuring Axes Example" name="configuring-axes-example" type="generated" /%}

### Series and Data

A chart can contain multiple series, which are provided in the `series` array.

Every series has its own [series options](/options/#reference-AgChartOptions-series), but they all share a common set of configurations.
These include a `type` as well as `_Key` properties to connect to the visualisation to the data, such as `xKey` and `yKey` for cartesian charts.

```js format="snippet"
{
    data: [
        { year: '2001', women: 25 },
        { country: '2003', value: 26 },
    ],
    series: [
        {
            type: 'bar',
            xKey: 'year',
            yKey: 'women',
        },
    ],
}
```

### Axes

Cartesian charts can use [Categorical](./axes-types/#category), [Numerical](./axes-types/#number), [Time](./axes-types/#time), or [Logarithmic](./axes-types/#log) axes.
These do not need to be configured unless customisation is required.

Use `axes.position` to control the position of the axis, and `axes.type` to define the type.

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        x: {
            type: 'category', // category | grouped-category | number | log | time | ordinal-time
            position: 'bottom', // top | right | bottom | left
        },
        y: {
            type: 'number', // category | grouped-category | number | log | time | ordinal-time
            position: 'left', // top | right | bottom | left
        },
    },
}
```

Polar charts such as the [Radar Line Series](./radar-line-series), use [Polar Axes](./radar-line-series/#customisation) which have similar options.

_Note: All of the axes must be specified in order for these changes to take effect._

### Secondary Axes

Charts can have more than one axis in each direction, allowing for series with different domains to be displayed on the same chart.

Use the axes `keys` property to link the series `_Key` property to the axis.

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        x: {
            type: 'category',
            position: 'bottom',
        },
        y: {
            type: 'number',
            position: 'left',
        },
        ySecondary: {
            type: 'number',
            position: 'right',
        },
    },
}
```

### Axis Intervals

The [Axis Interval](./axes-intervals/) determines which axis labels, grid lines and ticks are shown along the axis.
Intervals can be defined in [steps](./axes-intervals/#step), [absolute values](./axes-intervals/#values) or dynamically with [min/max spacing](./axes-intervals/#min--max-spacing).

Use `axes.interval` to control the interval of the axis.

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        y: {
            type: 'number',
            position: 'left',
            interval: { step: 5 }, // or { values: [0, 5, 10] } | { minSpacing: 50, maxSpacing: 100 }
        },
    },
}
```

### Axis Labels

[Axis labels](./axes-labels/) can be styled, rotated and configured to automatically avoid collisions.

Use `axes.label` to control the appearance of the axis labels.

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        y: {
            type: 'number',
            position: 'left',
            label: {
                minSpacing: 20,
                avoidCollisions: false, // enabled by default
                autoRotate: false, // enabled by default
            },
        },
    },
}
```

### Value Formatting

Data values are displayed in many places, such as series and axes labels. Use the chart-level or item-level `formatter` callbacks to set the format of these.

The `params` contain contextual information necessary to identify the item, and the callback should return a `String` object.

```js format="snippet"
formatter: function (params) {
    return params.value * 100 + '%';
}
```

## Data Elements

{% chartExampleRunner title="Enhancing Data Example" name="enhancing-data-example" type="generated" /%}

### Legend

[Legends](./legend/) are enabled by default on charts with more than one series.

Use the `legend` options to configure the legend position and size and to disable the series toggling functionality on legend click.

```js format="snippet"
{
    legend: {
        enabled: true, // defaults to true for charts with multiple series
        position: 'top', // 'bottom', 'right', 'left',
        toggleSeries: false,
    },
}
```

### Cross Lines

Use the `crossLines` array in the axis options to add `line` or `range` [Cross Lines](./axes-cross-lines/) along any axis.

```js format="snippet"
{
    axes: {
        y: {
            crossLines: [
                {
                    type: 'line',
                    value: 11,
                },
                {
                    type: 'range',
                    range: ['Jun', 'Sep'],
                },
            ],
        },
    },
}
```

### Conditional Styling

[Item Stylers](./stylers/#item-stylers) allow customisation of the visual appearance of series and markers based on certain conditions.

Use the `itemStyler` callback function to customise the appearance of a specific bar or marker:

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            marker: {
                itemStyler: ({ datum, xKey, fill, highlighted }) => {
                    return {
                        fill: datum[xKey] === 'Jul' ? (highlighted ? 'lime' : 'red') : fill,
                    };
                },
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

## Layout and Styling

{% chartExampleRunner title="Customising Charts Example" name="customizing-charts-example" type="generated" /%}

### Fills and Strokes

Each series type has its own [series specific styling options](/options/#reference-AgChartOptions-series) depending on the visualisation, with the most common properties being `fill` and `stroke`.

[Series Fills](./fills) can be a solid colour, [a gradient](./fills/#gradients), [a pattern](./fills/#patterns) or [an image](./fills/#images).

Strokes can have a `stroke` colour, as well as a `strokeWidth` and a `lineDash[]` array.

### Marker Shape

[Markers](./markers/) can be customised to change their [shape, size, and style](./markers/#marker-shape-size-and-colour).

Use the `series.marker` property to customise markers.

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            stroke: 'maroon',
            marker: {
                shape: 'square', // defaults to 'circle'
                size: 20,
                fill: 'red',
                stroke: 'maroon',
            },
        },
    ],
}
```

### Chart Elements

Various chart elements can be customised to change their [Fills & Borders](./fills-borders) as well as padding and corner radius.

### Themes

AG Charts comes with 5 [Themes](./themes/), each available in light or dark mode.

Use the `theme` property to set the theme:

```js format="snippet"
{
    theme: 'ag-default', // or 'ag-sheets', 'ag-polychrome', 'ag-material', 'ag-vivid'
}
```

Themes can also be customised or created with the [Theming API](/themes-api/).

Use `params` to set styles across the whole chart, `palette` to define the colour palette used for the datapoints, and `overrides` to change a specific default within the base theme.

```js
const customTheme = {
    palette: {
        //these are used by each series in rotation
        fills: ['#5C2983', '#0076C5', '#21B372', '#FDDE02', '#F76700', '#D30018'],
        strokes: ['#881008'],
    },
    params: {
        // these are used by all chart elements unless explicitly overridden
        fontFamily: 'Georgia, serif',
        fontSize: 16,
    },
    overrides: {
        //used as default for all series types
        common: {
            title: {
                fontSize: 24,
            },
        },
        bar: {
            //used for bar series only
            series: {
                label: {
                    enabled: true,
                    color: 'white',
                },
            },
        },
    },
};
```

### Chart Size

The chart will dynamically [auto-size](./layout/#chart-size) to the size of its container element, defaulting to a minimum width and height of 300px.

Use the `width`/`minWidth` and `height`/`minHeight` options if a fixed size is required:

```js format="snippet"
{
    width: 800,
    height: 600,
}
```

## Interactivity

{% chartExampleRunner title="User Interactions Example" name="user-interactions-example" type="generated" /%}

### Tooltips

[Tooltips](./tooltips/) are enabled by default. Their content is based on the data values and keys of the series.

[Tooltip modes](./tooltips/#tooltip-modes) include a shared tooltip which includes the values for all series, and a compact tooltip for smaller charts.

```js format="snippet"
{
    tooltip: {
        mode: 'shared', // or 'single' or 'compact'
    },
}
```

Use the `tooltip.renderer` callback function to customise the content of the tooltip or create a [Custom Tooltip](./tooltips/#using-custom-tooltips):

```js format="snippet"
{
    tooltip: {
        renderer: (params) => {
            return '<div class="custom-tooltip">' + params.datum[params.xKey] + '</div>';
        },
    },
}
```

### Highlighting Hovered Data

Items and Series are [highlighted](./series-highlighting/) when hovered.

Use `highlight` options to [customise](./series-highlighting/#customisation) highlighted and unhighlighted items and series:

```js format="snippet"
{
    highlight: {
        // Attributes that apply to the highlighted item only.
        highlightedItem: {
            strokeWidth: 5,
        },
        // Attributes that apply to all other series.
        unhighlightedSeries: {
            opacity: 0.2,
        },
    },
}
```

### Handling Events

Callbacks are provided for [Series](./events/#series-events), [Legend](./events/#legend-events) and [Chart](./events/#chart-events) events.
These include clicks, double-clicks and zoom events.

Register `listeners` to respond to [Events](./events/) on the chart.

```js format="snippet"
{
    // Charts Events
    listeners: {
        click: (params) => {
            console.log('click');
        },
    },
    // Series Events
    series: [
        {
            listeners: {
                seriesNodeClick: (params) => {
                    console.log('node clicked');
                },
            },
        },
    ],
    // Legend Events
    legend: {
        listeners: {
            legendItemClick: (params) => {
                console.log('item clicked');
            },
        },
    },
}
```

### Saving and Restoring State

The dynamic [State](./api-state/) of the chart can be saved and restored. This includes the [Zoom](./zoom/), [Annotation](./annotations/) and [Series Visibility](./legend/#series-visibility-toggling).

Use the `getState()` and `setState()` APIs to retrieve and restore the current state of the chart.

```js
function saveState() {
    const newState = chart.getState();
    // save to database...
}

function restoreState() {
    // retrieve state from database...
    chart.setState(state);
}
```

You can also use the `initialState` property to set the [initial state](./api-state/#initial-state) of the chart when it loads.

```js format="snippet"
{
    initialState: {
        zoom: {
            rangeX: {
                start: {
                    __type: 'date',
                    value: new Date('2021-01-01').getTime(),
                },
            },
        },
    },
}
```

## Enterprise Features {% enterpriseIcon /%}

AG Charts comes in two forms:

-   **AG Charts Community**: Free for everyone, including production use - no licence required.
-   **AG Charts Enterprise**: Requires a licence to use in production. Free to test locally, but requires a [trial](./community-vs-enterprise/#request-an-enterprise-bundle-trial-licence) to test in production.

Import the `ag-charts-enterprise` package to access Enterprise features.

Learn more on the [Community vs. Enterprise](./community-vs-enterprise/) page.

_All Enterprise features are marked with an {% enterpriseIcon /%} in our docs._

{% chartExampleRunner title="Enterprise Features Example" name="enterprise-features-example" type="generated" /%}

In the above example you can [Zoom](./zoom/) by scrolling over the chart, or by using the [Navigator](./navigator/) or [Context Menu](./context-menu/).

### Zoom {% enterpriseIcon /%}

To enable [Zooming](./zoom/), set the `zoom` property to `true`.

```js format="snippet"
{
    zoom: {
        enabled: true,
    },
}
```

See the [Zoom Options](/options/#reference-AgChartOptions-zoom) for further customisation options, including enabling specific zoom methods, controlling the anchor point, scrolling step, and many more.

The y-axis will automatically adjust to fit the visible items. This is controlled by the `autoScaling` option.

```js format="snippet"
{
    zoom: {
        autoScaling: {
            enabled: true,
        },
    },
}
```

### Navigator {% enterpriseIcon /%}

Zooming and Panning can be done with the [Navigator](./navigator/).

A [Mini Chart](./navigator/#mini-chart) can also be displayed within the Navigator to give additional context.

```js format="snippet"
{
    navigator: {
        enabled: true,
        miniChart: {
            enabled: true,
        },
    },
}
```

### Context Menu {% enterpriseIcon /%}

The [Context Menu](./context-menu/) provides [Zoom](./zoom/) and [Download](./api-download/) functionality, as well as allowing element specific [custom actions](./context-menu/#custom-actions).

```js format="snippet"
{
    contextMenu: {
        enabled: true,
        items: [
            {
                showOn: 'always',
                label: 'Say hello',
                action: () => log('Hello world!'),
            },
            'separator',
            {
                showOn: 'series-node',
                label: 'Say hello to a node',
                action: ({ datum, yKey }) => {
                    console.log(`Hello ${yKey} in ${datum.month}!`);
                },
            },
        ],
    },
}
```

### Annotations {% enterpriseIcon /%}

[Annotations](./annotations/) allow end users to add trend-lines and text annotations to aid in data analysis and markup.

These are available in all Cartesian charts and can be enabled with `annotations.enabled = true`.

## Specialized Charts {% enterpriseIcon /%}

### Financial Charts {% enterpriseIcon /%}

Build interactive [Financial Charts](./financial-charts/) featuring advanced annotations with minimal configuration.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Financial Charts Showcase" name="financial-charts-showcase" type="generated" /%}

To create a financial chart, simply provide your financial data:

{% if isFramework("javascript") %}

```js format="snippet"
const options = {
    data: getData(),
};

AgCharts.createFinancialChart(options);
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("react") %}

```jsx format="reactHooks"
const [options, setOptions] = useState({
    data: getData(),
});

return <AgFinancialCharts options={options} />;
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("angular") %}

```ts format="snippet"
@Component({
    selector: 'app-root',
    standalone: true,
    imports: [AgFinancialCharts],
    template: `<ag-financial-charts [options]="options"></ag-financial-charts>`,
})
export class AppComponent {
    public options: AgFinancialChartOptions;
    constructor() {
        this.options = {
            data: getData(),
        };
    }
}
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("vue") %}

```js format="snippet"
template: `<ag-financial-charts:options="options"/>`,
components: {
    'ag-financial-charts': AgFinancialCharts,
},
data() {
    return {
        options: {
            data: getData(),
        },
    };
}
```

{% /if %}

### Maps {% enterpriseIcon /%}

The [Map Series](./maps/) types enable visualising geographic data in different ways, using [GeoJSON](https://geojson.org/) data.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Map Kitchen Sink Example" name="map-kitchen-sink" type="generated" /%}

To create a map chart, provide a [Map Topology](./map-topology/) in [GeoJSON](https://geojson.org/) format and link the correct property in the `properties` key:

```js format="snippet"
{
    series: [
        {
            type: 'map-shape',
            topology: {
                type: 'FeatureCollection',
                features: [
                    {
                        type: 'Feature',
                        geometry: {
                            type: 'Polygon',
                            coordinates: [
                                /*...*/
                            ],
                        },
                        properties: {
                            name: 'United Kingdom',
                            code: 'GB',
                        },
                    },
                ],
                // ...
            },
            data: [
                { country: 'United Kingdom', population: 67330000 },
                { country: 'France', population: 67500000 },
                // ...
            ],
            idKey: 'country',
            topologyIdKey: 'name', //default value
        },
    ],
}
```

### Gauges {% enterpriseIcon /%}

[Radial Gauges](./radial-gauge/) and [Linear Gauges](./linear-gauge/) display a single data point within a predefined range using a Radial or Linear scale.

{% chartExampleRunner title="Radial Gauge Example" name="simple-radial-gauge" type="generated" /%}

Provide a `type`, `value` and `scale` to create a gauge with the `createGauge` API:

{% if isFramework("javascript") %}

```js format="snippet"
const options = {
    type: 'radial-gauge', // or 'linear-gauge'
    container: document.getElementById('myChart'),
    value: 80,
    scale: {
        min: 0,
        max: 100,
    },
};

AgCharts.createGauge(options);
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("react") %}

```jsx format="reactHooks"
const [options, setOptions] = useState({
    type: 'radial-gauge', // or 'linear-gauge'
    value: 80,
    scale: {
        min: 0,
        max: 100,
    },
});

return <AgGauge options={options} />;
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("angular") %}

```ts format="snippet"
@Component({
    selector: 'app-root',
    standalone: true,
    imports: [AgGauge],
    template: `<ag-gauge [options]="options"></ag-gauge>`,
})
export class AppComponent {
    public options: AgRadialGaugeOptions;
    constructor() {
        this.options = {
            type: 'radial-gauge', // or 'linear-gauge'
            value: 80,
            scale: {
                min: 0,
                max: 100,
            },
        };
    }
}
```

{% /if %}

{% if isFramework("vue") %}

```ts format="snippet"
template: `<ag-gauge :options="options"/>`,
components: {
    'ag-gauge': AgGauge,
},
data() {
    return {
        options: {
            type: 'radial-gauge', // or 'linear-gauge'
            value: 80,
            scale: {
                min: 0,
                max: 100,
            },
        },
    };
}
```

{% /if %}
